{"answer":"The council will set a short‑term exemption to last only for the trip on which it is granted – that is, it expires when the fisher returns to shore after that day’s catch. If the council decides a longer period is needed (for example a week in case of a very bad season), it will note the exact end date in the ledger. The ledger entry will include the fisher’s name, the date of the exemption request, the duration agreed, and a stamp of the council’s approval. This way every exemption is clearly bounded and can’t be used beyond the agreed window.","revised_policy":"In our village, a fisher may keep no more than one unit of fish per trip, defined as one fish of the main species that is at least 35 cm long. The fisher must measure each fish with a personal measuring tape, count the qualifying fish, and place exactly that amount in a sealed bag. Any fish caught beyond one unit is released back into the lake. If a fisher needs more due to scarcity, they may request a short‑term temporary exemption; this request must be approved by a simple majority of the council and recorded in the ledger with an explicit end date or duration. All excess fish caught under an approved exemption are returned to the lake after consumption, with the fisher noting the exemption in the ledger. Penalties for violations are collected and deposited into the communal pot for community use.
1. Before setting out, each fisher notes the 1‑unit marker on the measuring tape, records the date, and signs the ledger.
2. During the trip, the fisher captures fish, measures each to confirm it is at least 35 cm, counts the qualifying fish, and sets aside exactly one such fish in a sealed bag.
3. Any fish caught beyond the one unit is placed in a separate bucket.
4. An observer appointed by the council checks the bag’s contents against the fisher’s count before the bag is sealed, signs the ledger, and confirms the fisher has kept only one unit.
5. The fisher seals the bag and returns to shore, depositing the excess back into the shared bucket.
6. At the daily communal meeting, the ledger is reviewed; any exemption requests are read aloud, the council votes by show of hands, and a majority vote approves the exemption, recording the duration or end date in the ledger.
7. The fisher documents the approval in the ledger and records the excess fish caught under the exemption.
8. The council holds a review after each season to adjust limits if needed.","revised_operationalization":"After the bag is sealed, the fisher places any excess fish in the shared bucket. At the end of the trip, a council‑appointed observer or designated volunteer will:
1) Verify the bucket’s contents;
2) Release each fish back into the lake gently, ensuring they remain alive;
3) Record the release action, the number of fish returned, and note whether the release is part of an approved exemption with its expiry date, in the ledger;
4) Return to the shore within 30 minutes of the fisher’s arrival to minimize stress on the fish. This process is repeated daily for all trips and ensures the community’s shared resource is respected.","reasoning":"I added the duration clause to answer the question directly, keeping the rest of the policy unchanged. The operationalization is updated only to include recording the exemption duration so it is clear when the exemption ends."}

