{"answer":"We appoint the elder and the scribe by consensus at the weekly village meeting. The elder is usually the fisher with the most experience and a history of fair play; the scribe is someone literate, impartial, and trusted by the others. Eligibility for either role is that the villager must be an adult, have a good standing in the community (no recent violations), and be able to read and write (for the scribe) or be respected for skill and honesty (for the elder). They are elected by a simple majority of those present, and after a set rotation period a new candidate may be chosen. This keeps the positions accountable and reflective of community trust.


Policy: "Each fisher may take a maximum of 0.75 units per trip; surplus fish returned are redistributed to those below the 1‑unit daily consumption need. Any remaining shortfall is met by the communal Reserve Balance or collective sharing. The Reserve Balance is replenished by surplus fish that remain after redistribution and by voluntary extra contributions (fish or labor) from fishers who catch more than needed. Violations are penalized by the community elder after a consensus vote."


Operationalization: 1. Every fisher carries a calibrated net‑scale that shows the catch weight immediately after setting the net. 2. If the scale shows more than 0.75 units, the fisher must return the excess to the lake before finishing the trip; all returned fish are counted as surplus. 3. After each fishing round, the designated elder collects the returned surplus, weighs it on communal scales, and records the total in the communal ledger. 4. The scribe, elected by consensus, maintains the ledger in the village meeting house and posts a copy on the wall board; during the daily meeting the ledger is read aloud and any villager may inspect it. 5. The elder calculates each fisher’s deficit relative to the 1‑unit survival need and distributes the surplus proportionally to those deficits, recording the allocation on the ledger. 6. If the surplus is insufficient to cover all deficits, the elder draws from the Reserve Balance (tracked as a line item in the ledger); any remaining shortfall after the reserve is exhausted leads the elder to order surplus catchers to contribute part of their next day’s catch or provide an extra labor shift, recording the adjustment on the ledger. 7. Any excess surplus after redistribution that is not needed to cover deficits is added to the Reserve Balance. 8. When a violation is discovered, the elder convenes a quick meeting of all active fishers, discusses the excess amount, and the community votes on an appropriate penalty (monetary contribution or extra fishing shift). The elder records the penalty in the ledger and collects any monetary contribution into the communal pot; if the penalty is an extra shift, the elder assigns the fisher to the next scheduled trip and notes this on the ledger. 9. The elder ensures no fisher may leave the next trip until the penalty is recorded, maintaining transparency and social enforcement.","revised_policy":"Policy: "Each fisher may take a maximum of 0.75 units per trip; surplus fish returned are redistr