ntribution into the communal pot; if the penalty is an extra shift, the elder assigns the fisher to the next scheduled trip and notes this on the ledger. 9. The elder ensures no fisher may leave the next trip until the penalty is recorded, maintaining transparency and social enforcement.","reasoning":"I kept the policy and operationalization unchanged because the question was about the selection of elder and scribe, not the rules themselves. I explained the election by consensus, eligibility criteria, and rotation to maintain trust and accountability."}

{"answer":"The elder enforces the order by first noting the surplus contribution that the fisher is owed on the communal ledger.  On the next scheduled trip the elder calls the fisher to the dock and says: “You must return the extra portion of your catch or do an extra labor shift before you may load your net.”  If the fisher agrees, the elder records the act as a fulfilled obligation and the fisher may return to the lake.  If the fisher refuses or cannot comply, the elder brings the matter to a quick, standing‑up meeting of all active fishers.  In that meeting the elder presents the ledger entry, the amount owed, and asks the group to vote on a fair penalty—usually a short extra shift or a small monetary contribution.  A simple majority vote resolves the dispute; the elder records the chosen penalty and collects any monetary payment into the communal pot.  If the fisher still does not comply after the vote, the elder may temporarily bar the fisher from the next trip until the penalty is paid and recorded, ensuring that the community’s resource is used fairly.  This process is repeated only if a new violation occurs; the elder keeps the ledger transparent so that everyone sees that the fisher’s debt is recorded and that the community’s agreement is upheld.

Thus, enforcement relies on the elder’s authority to assign and record duties, on the community’s collective voting to decide a penalty, and on the ledger’s transparency to hold fishers accountable.  Refusal is treated as a violation and is handled by the same voting mechanism used for any rule breach.
